{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793653392,"nonce":"lfGINPiIlw9tt6w8yGo1lHE27Jk=","message":"Owner requirements, 2026-11-02: every block follows the same CSS-first styling and prop-binding reactivity contracts as blinc_cn. Use Signal, Computed and Effect. Document component classes so consumers can restyle the blocks. These are mandatory implementation and acceptance requirements for all blocks and any new CN atoms, not optional conventions.\n\nStyling contract\n- Builders expose semantic variants, classes and bound attributes; CSS rules and theme variables define appearance and visual states, including hover, focus, disabled, loading, selected, expanded and invalid states where applicable.\n- Derived values describe semantic data, not Rust-selected palettes or visual-state styling. Motion uses declarative motion containers and CSS transitions/keyframes with theme timing tokens. A missing CSS capability is addressed in the shared engine rather than replaced with per-block Rust styling logic.\n- Reuse CN atoms and their styling contracts, with a consistent namespace for block-owned classes and variables. Consumer overrides compose through the normal stylesheet cascade.\n\nReactivity contract\n- Bind runtime props in place through the existing prop-binding channel. Accept reactive inputs through IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e; use Signal for mutable values, Computed for derived values, and Effect for subscriptions/binding integration with proper ownership and cleanup.\n- Status, labels, counts, progress, disabled values and other nonstructural updates must not rebuild the block or its surrounding workflow. Membership/structure changes use existing keyed regions and conditional composition; retained rows bind their live fields independently.\n- Preserve focus, selection, scroll position and stable identity through updates. Reuse existing binding and lifecycle machinery instead of introducing another reactive layer.\n\nRequired restyling documentation for each block\n- List the root and part classes, modifier classes, relevant pseudo-classes and state attributes, including supported values and the slot/element each selector targets.\n- List public CSS custom properties and the inherited theme tokens/defaults they reference.\n- Describe custom-content slots, inherited CN classes, and separately mounted overlay/portal roots that affect selector scope.\n- Include a minimal CSS override example, theme stylesheet registration/override order, and examples for relevant interactive states.\n- Treat documented selectors and variables as a supported customization surface. Changes require migration notes, not silent selector churn.\n\nDefinition of done: the block ships with its styling/prop contract, restyling reference and compile-checked usage example; scoped tests verify in-place reactive updates and representative consumer CSS overrides, including interactive states and theme changes. A block is incomplete until these docs and checks exist.","files":null}]}